<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Old Folks at Home</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1851">1851</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Old Folks at Home&quot;, also known as &quot;Swanee River&quot;, was commissioned from Stephen Foster in 1851 by E. P. Christy for his minstrel troupe, Christy's Minstrels. Christy also asked to be credited as its author and was named as such on early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printings</a> of the sheet music, so Foster gained little directly from its success, though he went on receiving royalties.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Foster had written most of the words but could not settle the name of the river in the opening line, and asked his brother Morrison for a Southern river with a name of two syllables. By Morrison's account, Stephen turned down Yazoo and Pedee, and the two looked over an atlas until they found the Swanee, a little river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> emptying into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>. Foster never saw the Suwannee River or visited Florida. The song is written in the first person, from the perspective and in the dialect of an enslaved African, at a time when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was legal in 15 of the states.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The narrator's longing for the old plantation has been criticised as romanticising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-e-b-du-bois">W. E. B. Du Bois</a>, in The Souls of Black Folk (1903), read the longing for the old folks at home as a longing for the people and traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. At public performances another word is usually sung in place of the offensive word for Black people in Foster's lyrics, as brothers was at the dedication of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> state capitol building in 1978, and the dialect pronunciation has long been disregarded.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> made it its state song in 1935, replacing Florida, My Florida, and it was traditionally sung at the inauguration of a governor. Charlie Crist left it out of his inauguration ceremony in 2007 and encouraged a contest for a new song. In 2008 the Florida Legislature adopted Florida (Where the Sawgrass Meets the Sky) as the state anthem and kept Old Folks at Home as the state song, with a revised text approved by scholars at the Stephen Foster Memorial, now set by statute.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonin-dvorak">Antonín Dvořák</a> arranged the song for baritone, chorus and orchestra in 1894, and his Humoresque No. 7 is musically similar. Early successful recordings included those of Len Spencer in 1892, the Haydn Quartet in 1904 and Alma Gluck in 1915. Bing Crosby sang it in the film <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> in 1935, and Ray Charles reworked it in 1957 as Swanee River Rock.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Old Folks at Home” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
